 1 big thing: MAGA opens new front on Dario 
 
 By Maria Curi and Marc Caputo 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photo: Jason Henry/Bloomberg via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump's allies are targeting Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei as the face of AI "doomerism" and a founding father of the effective altruism movement, which has come under increasing political fire.

 Why it matters: The attacks signal that Anthropic could remain a Trump target ahead of the midterm elections. Trump surrogates see Amodei as an easy foil because of his politics and habit of highlighting worst-case scenarios for AI's impact on society, sources told Axios.

 For investors, it's a worrisome proposition as the company prepares for what's expected to be a record-setting IPO. Behind the scenes: Obtained by Axios, a memo that began circulating within the White House this week seeks to paint effective altruism as a fringe, cultish collective out of touch with mainstream America. It places Amodei at the foundation of the movement, which defines itself as an effort to maximize the benefits of philanthropy.

 Effective altruism "built the AI-doom pipeline," states the memo, which was penned by a Trump political adviser. Critics of the movement have called out its obsession with catastrophic AI risk, animal welfare (including musings on shrimp consciousness ) and other values they deem far from the U.S. mainstream. The memo says it prioritizes "foreigners over citizens, shrimp over families, future hypothetical people over the living, and — on the current agenda — possible machine minds over Americans." It names Amodei as one of the people who "built the [EA] network" as well as his sister and Anthropic president Daniela Amodei, whose husband once led a philanthropy dedicated to AI safety.

 The other side: Anthropic has been working to distance itself from effective altruism, which has ties to the AI research community. 

 In a 2025 Wired story, Daniela Amodei says "I don't identify with that terminology," and early employee Amanda Askell — a philosopher by training who helps develop Claude's constitution — says it's "not a theme of the organization." Amodei has also insisted he's not a member in press interviews and has touted the benefits of AI. Between the lines: Drafted with Trump in mind, the memo underscores the profound philosophical and political differences between MAGA and Silicon Valley elites, a divide that has made Anthropic's relationship with the White House rocky.

 Months ago, when some on Anthropic's team inquired about Amodei meeting with the president, White House officials advised against it because "Dario's a little too weird" for Trump, one senior official told Axios. It's not just MAGA. Tech execs and Democrats also criticize the EA movement and see some safety warnings — especially about the risk AI will wipe out humanity — as adding to a growing populist backlash against the technology.

 "What they believe is crazy. And it's dangerous," one Democratic donor and tech entrepreneur invested in an AI company said. Catch up quick: The EA movement includes many participants and players, and AI safety concerns are one of many areas of focus. Peter Singer, an animal rights advocate and philosopher who's been closely affiliated with its tenets, has long called for the vast sums spent in philanthropy to be put to better use.

 Reality check: Some in the administration have previously put aside gripes with Anthropic, mainly due to its role leading the AI frontier and personnel changes .

 The bottom line: OpenAI catching up technologically could make it easier for Trump acolytes to go after the embattled company again.

 
 
