 2. Meta brings a viral assistant to the keychain 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo: Ina Fried/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Meta yesterday debuted Muse Charm, a new, keychain-sized AI handheld device it will use to bring its viral Muse assistant to the masses.

 Why it matters: Meta has raced ahead of OpenAI , Apple and other companies that are working on a new crop of small AI devices, revving up the massive interest Muse has generated in the market.

 "We've packed a lot of technology in this little guy," chief executive Mark Zuckerberg said during his Meta Connect keynote yesterday. Driving the news: The Muse Charm will be available for purchase by December, and the company is the first tech giant to unveil its plans for an entirely new category of device. 

 OpenAI has spent billions to build a hardware division, so Meta's advance on the category is likely to stun many analysts and investors. Meta has also poured billions into hardware, most notably VR, an effort that most analysts have written off as a waste. Zuckerberg has long held that a convergence of those investments with AI could make the bet pay off. Catch up quick: In addition to Charm, Meta announced new Muse capabilities including live video chat, real-time voice conversations with custom voice options and an email address for each person's agent.

 Beyond Muse, Meta used the event to debut a range of new hardware along with new privacy options that could make the gear more palatable to the mainstream, including audio-only glasses and updates to other lines. Keep reading . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 3. Hands-on with Meta&#x27;s next VR headset 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Meta's new virtual reality glasses take a big step toward being something an average person might be willing to wear, ditching much of the bulk as well as the need for handheld controllers.

 You can watch me demonstrate them here ! Why it matters: Unlike Meta's Ray-Bans, its VR devices have struggled to break out of their gamer niche and into the mainstream.

 In order to slim them down, the new Meta VR Glasses require a wired "puck" that can be held or clipped onto a belt. It contains much of the device's computing horsepower, including its processor, battery and storage.

 Zoom in: I spent about half an hour on Tuesday using a prototype of the new specs, which are controlled through a combination of eye-tracking, hand gestures and voice commands.

 The glasses are designed to serve a range of purposes from watching a movie or immersive concert or playing games.

 I played a reimagined version of Beat Saber that cleverly remaps the music game for control via hand motions. I watched a clip from last year's NBA Finals. I used a trio of virtual monitors to do some "vibe coding" on a website, with one display showing the Muse terminal and another previewing the site itself. The headset can project a working keyboard onto any flat surface. Meta sees these VR glasses as a "general device," Meta VP of product Angela Chang told Axios. "It's not built for any specific use case per se." My thought bubble: It's the most practical combination I've seen of an Apple Vision Pro-class device in a headset I'd be willing to wear outside of the house.

 The bottom line: Meta said it has modest expectations for unit sales, but that success will look like attracting folks beyond gamers.

 
 
